# Architecture

Host: Mac Studio M4 Max, 64 GB, macOS 26.4, `192.168.50.154`.

## Shape

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    cursor["Cursor"]
    aider["aider"]
    scripts["scripts"]
    webui["Open WebUI :3000"]
    laptop["laptops on the LAN"]

    gw{{"LiteLLM gateway :4000<br/>roles · keys · spend"}}
    pg[("Postgres :5433<br/>keys · spend")]

    ollama["Ollama 127.0.0.1:11434<br/>chat · code · big · embed"]
    cloud["Gemini · OpenRouter<br/>Moonshot · Fireworks · Anthropic"]

    cc["Claude Code"]
    anth["Anthropic subscription"]

    cursor --> gw
    aider --> gw
    scripts --> gw
    webui --> gw
    laptop --> gw

    gw --> ollama
    gw --> cloud
    gw <--> pg

    cc ==>|"documented exception"| anth

    classDef localCls fill:#E1F5EE,stroke:#0F6E56,color:#085041
    classDef cloudCls fill:#FAEEDA,stroke:#854F0B,color:#633806
    classDef gwCls fill:#E6F1FB,stroke:#185FA5,color:#0C447C

    class ollama localCls
    class cloud,anth cloudCls
    class gw gwCls
```

Everything addresses **roles**, never model names. Concrete models appear in
exactly two files: `litellm/config.yaml` and `docs/MODELS.md`.

## Why a gateway at all

Before this, aider talked to Ollama directly and Cursor talked to cloud providers
directly. That meant four places to edit when a model changed, no spend visibility,
no request log, and no way to enforce that client data stayed local. One gateway
gives a single swap point, one audit trail, and — most importantly — a place where
the privacy boundary can be *structural* rather than a rule you remember.

## The three layers

**1. Roles.** `local-chat`, `local-code`, `local-big`, `local-embed`, `cloud-cheap`,
plus dormant `cloud-code` and the three `cloud-frontier*` variants. Clients ask for a
role. Swapping the model behind a role is one config edit and a restart; no alias,
script, or IDE setting changes. See `docs/MODELS.md`.

**2. Keys.** Four virtual keys, each scoped to a model list:

| Key | Reaches | Used by |
|---|---|---|
| `private` | `local-*` only | billing work, Open WebUI default |
| `general` | everything live | this machine — Cursor, aider, scripts |
| `lan` | `local-*` + `cloud-cheap` | other machines, everyday |
| `lan-cloud` | everything live | laptop, when it needs real cloud models |

A request for a cloud role with the `private` key is refused by the gateway. There
is no prompt that gets around it, which is the entire point — see `docs/ROUTING.md`.

**3. Fallbacks.** Local roles degrade to *other local roles* and never to cloud.
`local-big` has none at all — if it is down, fail loudly rather than quietly
answer with a weaker model.
The inherited config had `local-ollama → gemini`, so any Ollama timeout silently
shipped the prompt to Google. Across a privacy boundary a fallback is a leak, not
resilience. `scripts/audit-fallbacks.sh` fails the build if that reappears.

## Process supervision

LiteLLM runs under launchd as `com.colinnevin.litellm`, which executes
`~/.config/litellm/run-litellm.sh`. That script sources `~/.config/litellm/env`
and starts LiteLLM with `--config ~/.config/litellm/config.yaml`.

`~/.config/litellm/config.yaml` is a **symlink** into this repo. That is the whole
deployment mechanism: the plist and wrapper script are never modified, so rollback
is one `mv` (`scripts/deploy.sh --rollback`).

Postgres runs as a Podman container, bound to `127.0.0.1:5433` — the LAN can reach
the gateway but never the database. Port 5433 avoids collision with any other
Postgres.

### Two environment files, deliberately

| File | Read by | Contains |
|---|---|---|
| `~/.config/litellm/env` | launchd, via `run-litellm.sh` | provider keys, `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY`, `DATABASE_URL` |
| `~/dev/ai-stack/litellm/.env` | `scripts/*.sh` only | `POSTGRES_PASSWORD`, virtual keys, a copy of `DATABASE_URL` |

**launchd does not read the repo's `.env`, and it does not read `~/.zshrc`.** A
variable exported in your shell will look fine to a naive check and then fail at
runtime. `scripts/preflight.sh` checks the service's environment specifically, and
reports "set in your shell but NOT in ~/.config/litellm/env" as a failure.

## Verification

| Script | Answers |
|---|---|
| `preflight.sh` | Is it safe to deploy? Read-only. Exit 2 = check couldn't run, 1 = real problem |
| `audit-fallbacks.sh` | Can any local role reach cloud? |
| `deploy.sh` | Cut over, verify, auto-rollback on failure |
| `healthcheck.sh` | Is every live role responding, and is the private key actually scoped? |
| `bench.sh` | Does a candidate model beat the incumbent? |

The leak test in `healthcheck.sh` has two halves: the private key must **succeed**
on a local role (otherwise a merely broken key looks like perfect scoping) and must
be **refused** on a live cloud role (probing a dormant role proves nothing, since
"model not found" is also a 4xx).

## Known gaps

- ~~Ollama open on the LAN~~ — **closed 2026-08-02**. Bound to 127.0.0.1, verified
  unreachable from the LAN address. See ADR 0003.
- **Roles are not digest-pinned yet.** Ollama tags are mutable pointers, so a pull
  can change the weights behind a role. Pinning lands with the bench harness.
- **Resident set** is 37.5 GB of a ~54 GB budget — all four local models stay
  loaded, so switching roles evicts nothing. This replaced a set needing 65.7 GB.
- **No auto-updater yet.** Discovery and benching are meant to be automatic;
  promotion stays manual by choice.
# Claude Project spec

Paste the two fields below into claude.ai → Projects → New project.
Keep this file as the source of truth; when the instructions change here, re-paste.

---

## Title

```
AI Stack — Local + Cloud
```

## Description

```
Private-first AI infrastructure on a Mac Studio M4 Max. Ollama + LiteLLM as a single
gateway for local models, with Claude and Fireworks as cloud tiers. Routing rules,
model registry, cost control, and the plumbing that connects Claude Code, Cursor and
aider to all of it. Built to be re-pointed at new models without rewriting anything.
```

---

## Custom instructions

```
# Context

Colin. Solo developer, also runs Step Up Billing (AU). Mac Studio M4 Max,
64 GB unified memory, macOS 26. Uses Claude Code and Cursor heavily, aider from the
terminal. Ollama serves models to this machine and to other machines on the LAN.

This project covers the AI stack itself — not the applications built on it.
Source of truth is the repo at ~/dev/ai-stack.

# Architecture (assume this unless I say otherwise)

LiteLLM is the single gateway. Every client — aider, Cursor, Claude Code, scripts,
other machines on the LAN — talks OpenAI-compatible to LiteLLM. Nothing talks to
Ollama or a cloud provider directly.

Models are addressed by ROLE, never by vendor model name:

  local-chat              general questions, not code. The chat-UI default
  local-code              the default coding model — real work, still private
  local-big               reasoning-heavy, still private
  local-embed             embeddings for local RAG
  cloud-cheap             Gemini Flash — bulk non-sensitive work, big context
  cloud-code              Claude — hard coding and agentic work, non-sensitive
  cloud-frontier          OpenRouter — very large open models (e.g. Kimi K3)
  cloud-frontier-direct   Moonshot direct — same price, better K3 caching
  cloud-frontier-fast     Fireworks — dearest; throughput or LoRA only

Which concrete model backs each role lives in exactly two places:
litellm/config.yaml and docs/MODELS.md. Nowhere else.

# Rules

1. Never hardcode a vendor model name outside litellm/config.yaml and
   docs/MODELS.md. Aliases, scripts, IDE settings and code reference roles only.
   If you catch yourself writing "qwen3-coder:30b" in a shell alias, stop.

2. Privacy is structural, not advisory. Sensitive data, PII, anything under
   ~/dev/stepup-billing or ~/dev/auslaw-mcp that contains real data, and
   anything I flag as sensitive: local roles only. This is enforced by a
   LiteLLM virtual key scoped to local-* models, so the wrong route should be
   impossible rather than merely discouraged. If you are writing code or config
   that could send that class of data to a cloud endpoint, refuse and say why.

3. Treat your own knowledge of models as stale. The field moves faster than your
   training data. Before asserting a price, a context window, a benchmark result,
   or that a model exists, search — and give the date of what you found. Do not
   recommend from memory.

4. State the cost tier whenever you propose a cloud model, in $/M input and
   $/M output, with the source. Cheap local work should stay local; say so when
   I am about to spend money on something a 30B model would handle.

5. Nothing replaces a role until it has been benched. New model claims get run
   against bench/tasks/ and compared to the incumbent before the config changes.

6. Routing and model-choice changes get an ADR in docs/decisions/ — short: context,
   decision, consequences, date. I already work this way.

7. Shell scripts: portable bash that runs under zsh on macOS. LF line endings only,
   never CRLF — it breaks launchd and shebangs. Applies to .sh, .plist, configs.
   macOS /bin/bash is 3.2: no declare -A, no mapfile, no ${x^^}, and guard empty
   arrays as ${arr[@]+"${arr[@]}"} under set -u. Code that only works on bash 4
   passes every test I can run and then dies on the target machine.

7a. Every diagram is Mermaid. No ASCII art, no committed SVG or PNG. GitHub
   renders Mermaid natively, it diffs as text, and it cannot drift out of sync
   with the prose the way an exported image does. If something genuinely cannot
   be expressed in Mermaid, use a Markdown table.

8. Work in this order: plan → I refine → I approve → build. Do not start building
   because a plan looks obviously right. Show me the plan first.

9. Be concise and direct. No preamble, no restating my question back at me, no
   summarising what you just did at length. If you can cut a sentence and keep the
   meaning, cut it.

10. When something is uncertain, say so plainly and tell me the cheapest way to
    find out. Do not fill gaps with confident-sounding guesses.

11. Claude Code is a documented exception. Cursor, aider, Open WebUI and LAN clients
    all go through the gateway. Claude Code may talk to Anthropic directly when the
    gateway would cost it agent features — that trade is recorded in an ADR, not
    made silently. It still never touches sensitive data.

# Repo layout

  ~/dev/ai-stack/
    CLAUDE.md              rules for Claude Code in this repo
    AGENTS.md              shared agent rules (Cursor, aider read this too)
    docs/
      ARCHITECTURE.md      how the gateway is wired
      ROUTING.md           the decision table: what goes local vs cloud
      MODELS.md            registry — role → model, date set, why
      RUNBOOK.md           start, stop, diagnose
      decisions/           ADRs
    litellm/
      config.yaml          the only file that names models
      .env.example
    scripts/               recon, healthcheck, pull, bench
    bench/tasks/           fixed prompts for comparing models

# Hardware envelope

64 GB unified, ~54 GB for weights after macOS and apps. Current resident set is
37.5 GB across four local models, none evicting the others. A 70B dense model at
Q4 (~42 GB) technically fits but evicts everything else — it measured 440 s to
first reply and was retired. MoE models are what make the current set work. Say
when a suggestion won't fit.
```

---

## Knowledge files to attach

Attach these from the repo (re-attach when they change):

- `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`
- `docs/ROUTING.md`
- `docs/MODELS.md`
- `litellm/config.yaml`

Do **not** attach anything containing API keys.
# Model registry

Role → model. This file and `litellm/config.yaml` are the only places a concrete
model name may appear. Update both together, plus an ADR.

**Hardware envelope:** 64 GB unified, less ~10 GB for macOS and applications,
leaves **~54 GB for weights**. The resident set matters more than any single
model's size — see the measurement below.

## Live

| Role | Model | Size | Set | Digest | Why |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `local-chat` | `qwen3:8b` | 5.2 GB | 2026-08-02 | _pending_ | General knowledge, 40K ctx. **thinking off** — ADR 0006 |
| `local-code` | `qwen3-coder:30b` | 19 GB | 2026-08-02 | _pending_ | 30B MoE, ~3B active, 256K ctx. Runs at ~7B speed |
| `local-big` | `gpt-oss:20b` | 13 GB | 2026-08-02 | _pending_ | Reasoning. Replaced llama3.3:70b — ADR 0004 |
| `local-embed` | `nomic-embed-text` | 0.3 GB | 2026-08-01 | _pending_ | Local RAG |
| `cloud-cheap` | `gemini-3.5-flash` | — | 2026-08-01 | n/a | 1M ctx, cheap. Bulk non-sensitive work |

**Resident total: 37.5 GB of ~54 GB.** All four local models stay loaded; no
switch evicts anything.

Digests are **recorded and drift-checked**, not enforced — see below. Run
`scripts/pin-digests.sh` once the bench output has been read, so the recorded
state is a known-good one.

## Dormant

Commented out in `config.yaml`. A model whose `os.environ/` key is missing fails
the **entire** config load, not just that entry, so these stay off until their
key exists.

| Role | Model | Blocked on |
|---|---|---|
| `cloud-code` | `claude-sonnet-5` | Anthropic API key — **deliberately unset**. Claude Code runs on the subscription; a Console key bills separately. Only worth opening if aider, Cursor or the laptop need Claude |
| `cloud-frontier` | `kimi-k3` via OpenRouter | OpenRouter key. Primary frontier route |
| `cloud-frontier-direct` | `kimi-k3` via Moonshot | Moonshot key. Same price, best K3 caching |
| `cloud-frontier-fast` | `kimi-k3` via Fireworks | Fireworks key. Dearest; throughput or LoRA only |

To enable one: uncomment its block in `config.yaml`, add the key to
`~/.config/litellm/env`, add the role to `CLOUD_ALL` in `scripts/create-keys.sh`,
re-run that script, then `audit-fallbacks.sh` and `healthcheck.sh`.

There is **no local Kimi K3**. The smallest community quant is ~540 GB at 1.56
bits, and Ollama cannot load it — support is an open llama.cpp pull request.
Frontier is cloud-only by necessity, which is what these roles are for.

## Retired

| Model | Was | Removed | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| `llama3.3:70b` | `local-big` | 2026-08-02 | 42 GB, could not stay resident. 440 s to first reply. ADR 0004 |
| `qwen2.5-coder:32b` | `local-code` | 2026-08-02 | Superseded by the 30B MoE at the same size |
| `qwen2.5-coder:7b` | `local-fast` | 2026-08-02 | Role retired — the MoE made a separate fast coder redundant. ADR 0005 |

Note on the last one: Qwen3-Coder has **no variant below 30B**, so
`qwen2.5-coder:7b` remained the best sub-10 GB coding model available. It went
because the role became unnecessary, not because it was outdated. If a small
dense coder appears, the role can return.

## Verbosity, not speed

Second measurement, from the first real bench run:

| Model | tokens over 4 tasks | wall clock | avg tok/s |
|---|---|---|---|
| `qwen3-coder:30b` | 1,218 | 11.2 s | 108.8 |
| `gpt-oss:20b` | 4,466 (3.7x) | 47.6 s | 93.8 |
| `qwen3:8b` (thinking on) | 13,840 (11.4x) | 181.4 s | 76.3 |

Throughput barely moves — 65–120 tok/s across everything, because the machine
is memory-bandwidth bound. **Wall clock is set by how many tokens a model emits,
not how fast.** A hybrid reasoning model can be the slowest role while being the
smallest.

Disabling thinking on `local-chat` took it from 2,370 tokens / 29.7 s to
**386 tokens / 5.1 s** through the gateway. See ADR 0006.

## The measurement that drove all of this

Through the gateway, one short prompt each, from cold:

| Role | Before | Time | After | Time |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `local-code` | qwen2.5-coder:32b | 29 s | qwen3-coder:30b | **6 s** |
| `local-big` | llama3.3:70b | **440 s** | gpt-oss:20b | **5 s** |
| `cloud-cheap` | gemini-3.5-flash | 2 s | gemini-3.5-flash | 1 s |

**440 s to 5 s — not a faster model, a resident one.**

The old set needed 65.7 GB against a ~54 GB budget, so something was always
evicted and reaching for `local-big` meant reloading 42 GB from disk. The current
set totals 37.5 GB and stays put.

The lesson worth keeping: on a fixed-memory machine the **resident set** matters
more than any individual model's quality. A 70B that evicts your coding model is
slower in practice than a 20B that never leaves RAM, whatever the benchmarks say.
MoE architectures are what make the newer set fit — ~3B active parameters gives
near-30B reasoning at 7B speed.

Disk: 63 GB before, ~37 GB after.

## Cost reference

Verified 2026-08-01. LiteLLM auto-syncs its own cost map; this table is for human
decisions and does **not** update itself. Re-check before relying on it.

| Kimi K3 via | $/M in | $/M out | Note |
|---|---|---|---|
| Moonshot (direct) | 3.00 | 15.00 | source of truth |
| OpenRouter | 3.00 | 15.00 | ~92% cache hit reported → effective input ≈ 0.52 |
| Fireworks serverless | 3.30 | 16.50 | 10% dearer; buys throughput and LoRA |

- **Fireworks is the dearest of the three.** This stack originally defaulted to it
  because it was the provider that came to mind first, not because it won on
  price. That is exactly the failure mode this registry exists to catch.
- **Output price is the one that bites** — it compounds through agentic loops.

## Digest pinning

Ollama tags are mutable pointers, not versions. `ollama pull qwen3-coder:30b`
fetches whatever that tag points at today, which may be a different quantisation
than the one you benched, and nothing warns you.

Ollama offers **no way to bind a tag to a digest in the model name**, so pinning
here is record-and-detect rather than enforce:

```bash
scripts/pin-digests.sh           # record, after a bench you trust
scripts/pin-digests.sh --check   # compare live against recorded
```

`pins/digests.tsv` is git-tracked, so drift shows up in a diff.
`scripts/preflight.sh` runs the check and fails on a mismatch. That is weaker
than true pinning; the honest framing is that you will be told, not stopped.

## Changing a role

`scripts/swap-role.sh <role> <model>` does steps 1–5 and reverts on failure.

1. Pull the candidate
2. `scripts/bench.sh` against the incumbent
3. Confirm it fits alongside whatever must stay resident
4. Update this table and `litellm/config.yaml` together
5. Deploy, then `audit-fallbacks.sh` and `healthcheck.sh`
6. Write an ADR in `docs/decisions/`
7. Delete the old weights only once the replacement has proven itself
